WEBVTT

NOTE This transcript is the episode script as written and fact-checked, timed to the recording. Timestamps are approximate within a sentence.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.700
What's up guys, welcome back to CrimeTimeSnacks.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.200
In September 2026, the California parole board did something it did not have to do.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:18.100
It gave Lyle and Erik Menendez an early parole suitability hearing, tentatively scheduled for March 2027.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.800
One year earlier, that same board had turned both brothers down and told them to come back in August 2028.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:30.000
So next year, two men who were sentenced to die in prison are going to sit in front of a parole panel for the second time.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.100
Lyle is fifty-eight.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.100
Erik is fifty-five.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.200
They have been locked up since March of 1990.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.700
You've probably heard the headline version of this case.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.100
The Beverly Hills mansion, the shotguns, the spending spree, the trial on television.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.100
I covered the murders and the trials in an earlier episode, so I am only going to walk through that part once, and quickly.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.200
This episode is about everything that came after the verdict.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:01:03.200
Thirty years of appeals that went nowhere, a letter found in a dresser, a district attorney who wanted them resentenced and another one who fought it, a judge who cut their sentences, a parole board that said no, and now a date on the calendar.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.100
Let's unpack it.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:12.800
On August 20, 1989, Lyle and Erik Menendez killed their parents, Jose and Kitty, inside the family's five-million-dollar home in Beverly Hills.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.000
Lyle was twenty-one.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Erik was eighteen.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.300
Hold on to those two ages, because thirty-six years later they are the reason a parole hearing is possible at all.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:30.900
Two days before the killings, the brothers bought Mossberg shotguns at a Big 5 Sporting Goods store in San Diego, and Erik used a driver's license that had been stolen from a friend of Lyle's.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:37.800
After the murders they told police that unknown intruders were responsible, and Lyle suggested it might be business-related, a mob killing.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.400
Police looked into that at first.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.900
What turned detectives around was money and a confession.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:51.800
The brothers spent approximately seven hundred thousand dollars in the months after the killings, and they hired a computer expert to delete their father's recently updated will.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.500
Then Erik confessed to his psychologist, Jerome Oziel.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.500
Oziel told his mistress, Judalon Smyth.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.200
After the two of them broke up, Smyth went to the police.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.400
Lyle was arrested on March 8, 1990, outside the Beverly Hills mansion.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:09.200
Erik was in Israel for a tennis tournament, and he flew back to Los Angeles and turned himself in three days later.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:17.000
They were charged with two counts of first-degree murder with special circumstances for lying in wait, which made them eligible for the death penalty.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.200
On January 12, 1993, prosecutors announced that they would seek it.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.300
There were two trials, and the difference between them is the whole reason this case is still alive.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.800
So let's take them one at a time.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:32.900
The first trial did not begin until July 1993, and a good part of the wait was a fight over the therapy tapes.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:42.400
In August 1990, Judge James Albracht ruled the tapes of the sessions with Oziel were admissible, because Oziel said Lyle had threatened him, which broke doctor-patient privilege.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.000
That ruling was appealed, and the proceedings stalled for two years.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:54.100
In August 1992 the Supreme Court of California ruled that most of the tapes could come in, with one exception: the tape on which Erik discussed the murders.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.600
A grand jury indicted the brothers in December 1992.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.600
And here is a detail that gets lost.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:08.300
The special circumstance that the killings were committed for financial gain was deemed unsupported by the evidence, and it was excluded from the charges.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.700
The greed theory was argued at trial, but it was not what they were charged with.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.300
The first trial began in July 1993.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.400
Cameras were allowed in the courtroom, and it was broadcast on Court TV.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.400
The brothers had separate juries.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.300
Erik was represented by Leslie Abramson, and Lyle by Jill Lansing.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:32.800
The defense was that the brothers killed their parents out of fear for their lives, after a lifetime of abuse, especially sexual abuse by Jose.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.300
Lyle testified that his father began abusing him when he was six and stopped when he was eight.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:44.500
Erik testified that it continued until shortly before the murders, and that two weeks before the killings he told Lyle about it for the first time.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:48.800
Both of them testified that Jose had threatened to kill them if they did not keep it secret.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.200
They were not the only ones who took the stand on this.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:58.900
A cousin, Diane Vander Molen, testified that in the summer of 1976 Lyle confided in her that his father was sexually abusing him.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:03.300
She said she told Kitty, and Kitty sided with her husband and accused Lyle of lying.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.100
Under questioning she said she had never directly witnessed any abuse.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.800
Another cousin testified that Erik had told him about the abuse when they were children.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.500
That cousin's name was Andy Cano.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.200
Remember it.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.800
The prosecution's answer was that the abuse was fabricated.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:23.900
They told the jurors that nobody mentioned abuse until a legal defense was being put together seven months after the murders.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.900
It was absent from the taped sessions with the psychologist.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:36.900
On those tapes, the brothers complained about a dictatorial father and a suicidal mother, and according to Oziel's account they killed out of hatred and a desire to be free of their father's domination.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.400
The defense went after the psychologist.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:46.700
A defense consultant, Ann Burgess, called Oziel manipulative and controlling, and argued that he directed many of the statements on those recordings.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:54.700
And Judalon Smyth, the woman whose tip had led to the arrests, now testified for the defense, saying Oziel taped the sessions because he wanted to control the brothers.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:01.700
Prosecutors pointed out that she was the one who had gone to the police in the first place, and that she had changed sides after a tumultuous breakup.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:09.700
Prosecutor Pam Bozanich pointed to the shotguns bought in advance, and to a taped statement that the murders were planned beforehand.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:13.500
She played Lyle's 9-1-1 call for the jury to show how good an actor he was.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:19.900
And before the trial, Lyle had offered to bribe an ex-girlfriend to say that Jose had made sexual advances toward her.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.100
She went to the police.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.300
Lyle admitted to the scheme on the stand.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:27.700
After a month of deliberations, in January 1994, both juries deadlocked.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.000
And they split by gender.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:32.000
The female jurors leaned toward voluntary manslaughter.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.500
The male jurors pushed for first-degree murder.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.800
District Attorney Gil Garcetti chose to retry the brothers rather than offer a plea to lesser charges.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:42.300
The second trial looked very different.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.000
Judge Stanley Weisberg did not allow cameras this time.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.700
He said they would increase the risk that jurors would be exposed to commentary about the case outside the courtroom.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.200
It was also far less publicized.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.800
Erik testified for fifteen days about the abuse he alleged.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.200
Lyle did not testify at all.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:03.200
The judge limited some of the testimony about certain allegations, and he limited the defense to sixty-four witnesses.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:05.700
The cross-examination was blunt.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:13.000
Prosecutor David Conn challenged Erik's claim that the abuse continued when he was eighteen, with a car and enough money to leave home.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.600
He asked why Erik had not simply joined the Army.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:23.000
Erik said he would not have been safe from his father even there, because his father was, in his words, the most powerful man I've ever met.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.400
Erik also admitted that there were no witnesses to the sexual abuse he described.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.200
The experts split the same way the juries had.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:37.800
A psychology professor, John Wilson, testified for the defense that Erik showed symptoms of post-traumatic stress disorder that supported his account.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:45.200
A psychiatrist for the prosecution, Park Elliot Dietz, countered that there was no way to know that, because the allegations themselves were unproven.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.900
He added that Erik had not shown learned helplessness.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:51.000
He had bought guns and practiced with them at a shooting range.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.400
Then came the ruling that mattered most.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:59.600
Weisberg determined there was insufficient evidence that the brothers were in imminent danger when they killed their parents.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:03.000
Under California law, that was the door to manslaughter, and it closed.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.400
He allowed the defense to argue that they shot Jose in the heat of passion.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.300
He did not allow that argument for Kitty.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.200
The prosecution also went after credibility.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:21.000
Conn told the jury that Lyle had asked a friend to fabricate a story for the first trial, and police had found a seven-page letter, allegedly from Lyle, laying out how he wanted that friend to testify.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.200
There was a second letter, allegedly written to an ex-girlfriend, with instructions on how to testify.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:30.000
One line read: We will decide later around what date this incident occurred.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.600
The defense disputed that the letter was authentic.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.400
Even the penalty phase produced a side fight.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:43.500
Leslie Abramson reportedly told a defense witness, William Vicary, to edit his own notes of his meetings with Erik to take out potentially incriminating information.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.400
The district attorney's office decided not to open a criminal investigation.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.800
Both brothers filed motions for a mistrial over it, claiming ineffective representation.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:58.000
They were convicted of first-degree murder with special circumstances, and of conspiracy to murder.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:04.700
On July 2, 1996, Judge Weisberg sentenced them to life without the possibility of parole, the terms to run consecutively.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:08.900
So here is the argument their supporters have made for thirty years.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:12.200
One set of jurors heard the full abuse case and could not agree.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.600
The next jury heard less of it and convicted.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.800
And here is the argument on the other side.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:26.000
These are two defendants accused of coaching witnesses, and the judge who limited the abuse evidence still let them argue heat of passion, and the jury still said murder.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:28.000
Both of those things are in the record.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.000
Keep both in mind for what comes next.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.500
The brothers did not accept the verdict.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:37.400
They appealed, and then they kept appealing, and the record of those appeals is a straight line of losses.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:43.100
On February 27, 1998, the California Court of Appeal upheld the murder convictions.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:49.700
On May 28, 1998, the Supreme Court of California declined to review the case, which let that decision stand.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.600
Both brothers then filed habeas corpus petitions with the state supreme court.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.200
Those were denied in 1999.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.600
With nothing left to try in state court, they filed separate habeas petitions in federal district court.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:09.200
On March 4, 2003, a magistrate judge recommended denying them, and the district court adopted that recommendation.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.200
They appealed to the Ninth Circuit.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.800
On September 7, 2005, a three-judge panel denied both petitions.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:18.800
Every court that had looked at the case had reached the same answer.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.200
For a long time, that is exactly where it stood.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.100
Two men serving life without parole, with no legal road left in front of them.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.800
So what does a life sentence actually look like, year after year?

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.800
The record has some answers, and they are stranger and sadder than you might expect.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:39.900
On July 2, 1996, the same day he was sentenced to life without parole, Lyle married a woman named Anna Eriksson.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.100
The ceremony was done over the telephone, and his aunt Marta attended.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:49.700
They divorced on April 1, 2001, after Eriksson discovered he had been writing to another woman.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:54.900
In November 2003 he married Rebecca Sneed in a visiting area of Mule Creek State Prison.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.700
They had known each other for around ten years.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:59.100
They separated in 2024.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:04.100
On June 12, 1999, Erik married Tammi Saccoman in the waiting room of Folsom State Prison.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.600
She described the day later.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.700
She said: Our wedding cake was a Twinkie.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:08.500
We improvised.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.900
It was a wonderful ceremony until I had to leave.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:12.400
That was a very lonely night.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:20.500
In 2005 she self-published a book about the marriage, and she said she and her daughter drove a hundred and fifty miles every weekend to visit him.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:23.900
That same year Erik talked to People magazine about his sentence.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.900
He said: Tammi is what gets me through.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:27.700
I can't think about the sentence.

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:30.500
When I do, I do it with a great sadness and a primal fear.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.900
Their family points to what the brothers built in those years.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:40.800
According to relatives, they helped establish a hospice program, launched a beautification project, and created something called Green Space.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:46.700
The family's description is that it was all meant to remind people serving life sentences that their lives still have meaning.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:52.300
Remember that, because when the parole board finally got to look at their prison records, it did not see model prisoners.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:53.300
It saw something else.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:57.400
While the courts were saying no, the story never left television.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:02.600
In 1994 alone there was a CBS miniseries and a television film called Honor Thy Father and Mother.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:04.300
Court TV did an episode in 2000.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:06.500
Barbara Walters featured the brothers.

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:12.500
In 2017 there was a Lifetime movie with Courtney Love, and a Law and Order True Crime season produced by Dick Wolf.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:19.100
That same year, Erik gave telephone interviews from prison for an A&amp;E docuseries, and Lyle did one with Chris Cuomo for HLN.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.900
For most of those years the case was a punchline.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:25.500
The notes I worked from have an entire section on parodies and dark comedy.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:29.000
The brothers were the rich kids who shot their parents and bought Rolexes.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:32.400
One detail tells you how deep into the culture this went.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:38.100
The brothers can be seen sitting courtside at a Knicks game in the background of a Mark Jackson basketball card.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:42.600
In December 2018, eBay began terminating auctions that mentioned them in the listing.

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:44.600
Then the audience changed.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:50.400
In 2021, an ABC 20/20 special called Inside the Menendez Movement documented something nobody had predicted.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:52.700
The brothers had become popular on TikTok.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:12:03.200
Young adults, inside and outside the United States, who had not been born when the trials aired, were watching the old testimony about abuse and hearing it very differently than audiences had in 1993.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:08.200
In 2022, a Discovery Plus documentary asked outright whether the brothers had been misjudged.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:12.500
And in September 2024, Netflix released a Ryan Murphy series about the case.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:16.900
It was widely criticized for implying an incestuous relationship between the brothers.

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:22.600
The journalist Robert Rand, who wrote a book on the case, called that fantasy, with no credible evidence behind it.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:28.300
Erik answered it himself, in a statement posted through his wife's account on September 20, 2024.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:32.600
He called the series a dishonest portrayal, and he wrote: Is the truth not enough?

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:34.300
Let the truth stand as the truth.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.200
Five days later the family put out their own statement.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.600
They called the show a phobic, gross, anachronistic, serial episodic nightmare.

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.000
But here is the uncomfortable part.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:47.900
The series is widely credited with reigniting public interest in the case.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:52.600
Murphy himself said it was the best thing that had happened to the brothers in thirty years.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:59.400
And District Attorney George Gascón said that October that his office had received an influx of calls about Lyle and Erik Menendez.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:03.000
Legal experts who talked to Business Insider were careful about this.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.700
The series was not new evidence.

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:06.100
They called it a catalyst.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.400
It did things in the real world, too.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:14.100
The house in Beverly Hills where the murders happened saw an influx of tourists, some from outside the country.

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:23.100
Police took calls about trespassing, including people trying to get onto the property to recreate old photographs of the brothers, and a covered barricade and fencing went up.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:31.900
One of the actors, Cooper Koch, who played Erik, met both brothers at the Richard J. Donovan Correctional Facility as part of a prison reform meeting led by Kim Kardashian.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:37.200
And in February 2025, Lyle told TMZ that he and Erik were grateful the series had shed light on child abuse.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.400
So the brothers' own view of it moved, too.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:43.600
There was new evidence, though, or at least evidence that was being called new.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:44.600
It came in two pieces.

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:47.200
The first was a man named Roy Rosselló.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:53.600
He had been a member of Menudo, the boy band, which was signed to RCA Records while Jose Menendez was an executive there.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:14:03.700
On April 18, 2023, Rosselló appeared on NBC's Today show and said that when he was fourteen years old, he was drugged and raped by Jose Menendez while visiting the family's home in New Jersey.

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:10.500
The next month, Peacock released a documentary series built around his account, called Menendez plus Menudo: Boys Betrayed.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.600
The second was a letter, and this is where that name comes back.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:23.100
It appears to have been written by Erik in 1988, the year before the murders, to his cousin Andy Cano, the same cousin who testified at the first trial.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:24.900
In it Erik talks about the abuse.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:28.800
Robert Rand said he found it in 2018, in a dresser in Cano's bedroom.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:36.600
In May 2023, attorneys for the brothers filed a habeas corpus petition in Los Angeles County Superior Court built on those two things.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.800
The petition cited them as new evidence.

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:46.000
On October 3, 2024, George Gascón held a press conference and announced that his office was actively reviewing the petition.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:51.100
Three weeks later, on October 24, he announced he was asking the court to resentence the brothers.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:54.400
If a judge went along with it, they would be eligible for parole.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:59.000
What he actually said is worth hearing in full, because both sides quote half of it.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:04.900
He said: I do believe that the brothers were subjected to a tremendous amount of dysfunction in the home, and molestation.

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:11.500
And he also said: I don't believe that manslaughter would have been the appropriate charge given the premeditation that was involved.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:15.900
So he was not saying they were innocent, and he was not saying it was self-defense.

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:20.900
His recommendation rested on the length of time they had already served and on their behavior in prison.

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:23.600
His phrase was that they had paid their debt to society.

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:27.000
A resentencing hearing was set for December 11, 2024.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.000
It did not happen.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:31.300
At a hearing on November 25, it was pushed to the end of January.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:33.900
Then the Los Angeles fires pushed it to March 20.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:35.700
Then it was pushed again, to April.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.300
And there was a second track.

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:48.700
The brothers had also asked Governor Gavin Newsom for clemency, and in March 2025 he said on his own podcast that the parole board would hold risk-assessment hearings for each brother on June 13, to help him decide.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:52.700
In the middle of all that, Los Angeles elected a new district attorney.

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:56.200
Nathan Hochman took office, and he reversed Gascón's recommendation.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:58.800
He also filed an opposition to the habeas petition.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:00.700
Hochman did not soften it.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:04.200
He called the brothers' claims of self-defense self-serving lies.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:06.000
And he went straight at the letter.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:12.600
His argument was that the letter to Andy Cano was not credible, because it had not been used by either defense team at either trial.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:16.700
If a letter like that had existed in 1993, why did nobody put it in front of a jury?

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.900
That is the same position the original prosecutors took.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:25.500
They told the juries the abuse allegations were fabricated, and that the brothers killed out of hatred and for the estate.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:28.500
Thirty years later the office had come back to where it started.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:34.700
So by the spring of 2025, the question was in front of a judge with the county's top prosecutor arguing against it.

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:36.600
It is worth noticing what that means.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:40.400
The resentencing did not happen because the district attorney wanted it.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.400
By the end, it happened in spite of him.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:47.500
In May 2025, a judge resentenced Lyle and Erik Menendez to fifty years to life.

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:50.200
On paper that still sounds like forever.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:53.600
It is not, and the reason is those two ages I asked you to hold on to.

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:55.900
Lyle was twenty-one and Erik was eighteen.

00:16:55.900 --> 00:17:03.800
Under California law, because they were under twenty-six when they committed the murders, the new sentence made them immediately eligible for parole.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:06.100
Their family released a statement.

00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:10.300
They said: Erik and Lyle were once sentenced to life without the possibility of parole.

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:18.300
We will always be profoundly grateful that they have been resentenced and that our family can now imagine a future in which they are once again part of our everyday lives.

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:20.900
Eligible is not the same as released, though.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:22.300
A judge can change a sentence.

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:24.100
Only the parole board can open the door.

00:17:24.700 --> 00:17:28.100
The hearings came three months later, on consecutive days.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:30.400
On August 21, 2025, Erik went first.

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:35.900
The California Board of Parole Hearings denied him, citing ongoing rule violations and concerns about public safety.

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:40.500
Lyle was denied the following day, with the board referencing repeated cell phone infractions.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:47.400
Parole Board Commissioner Robert Barton cited prison violations, including the use of illegal cellphones and incidents of violence.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:50.300
One report said Erik had been linked to a prison gang at one time.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:53.400
The family had been telling the public these were model prisoners.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:57.400
The board read their files and said their conduct in prison did not make them suitable.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:01.500
This is the part of the story that does not fit neatly on either side.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:05.900
If you believe the brothers, the hospice program and Green Space are the real record.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:09.100
If you believe the board, the cellphones and the violence are.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:12.300
Both sets of facts are in the same file, about the same two men.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:14.700
The denial was for three years.

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:17.200
That put their next chance in August 2028.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:20.800
There was one more door, and it closed the following month.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:29.500
In September 2025, a Los Angeles Superior Court judge ruled on the brothers' petition for a new trial, the one built on the Rosselló allegation and the Cano letter.

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:31.400
Judge William Ryan denied it.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:40.300
He wrote: The evidence alleged here is not so compelling that it would have produced a reasonable doubt in the mind of at least one juror, or supportive of an imperfect self-defense instruction.

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:42.700
Read that sentence again, slowly.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:44.900
He is not saying the abuse did not happen.

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:51.100
He is saying that even if a jury had seen this evidence, it would not have changed the verdict on premeditated murder.

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:55.800
That is a narrower ruling than the headlines made it sound, and it is also a complete loss.

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:59.300
The convictions stand, and no court has granted them a new trial.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.100
Which brings us back to where we started.

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:10.500
In September 2026, weeks after the thirty-seventh anniversary of the murders, the parole board granted both brothers an early suitability hearing.

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:14.300
Instead of August 2028, it is tentatively scheduled for March 2027.

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:17.300
How does that happen after a three-year denial?

00:19:17.300 --> 00:19:22.900
According to the Orange County Register, it comes from two provisions in a California law called Proposition 9.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:31.500
Those provisions allow an inmate's next hearing to be moved up if there is a change in circumstances, or new information indicating the person may be suitable for parole.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:36.000
The family's statement thanked the board for recognizing the brothers' efforts.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:39.100
And it gave a reason for the urgency that has nothing to do with law.

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:49.900
They wrote that time together is something none of them can take for granted, particularly as the elderly members of the family, including the brothers' aunts Joan, Terry and Marta, grow older and face serious health challenges.

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:52.000
Not everyone is glad about it.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:20:02.800
A former Sacramento district attorney, Anne Marie Schubert, is leading a lawsuit against new Board of Parole Hearings guidelines, and she warns they will open a path to freedom for the state's worst violent criminals.

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:05.900
The Menendez hearing landed right in the middle of that fight.

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:10.300
Both brothers are held at the Richard J. Donovan Correctional Facility in San Diego.

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:15.100
So here is where the case stands, and here is what I think is worth watching next year.

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.000
The parole board is not going to retry 1989.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:21.300
It is not going to decide whether Jose Menendez abused his sons.

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:27.200
Gascón said he believed it, Hochman called it lies, and Judge Ryan ruled it would not have changed the verdict.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:29.200
None of that is the board's question.

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:31.600
The board's question is narrower and harder.

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:34.100
Are these two men, today, a danger to the public?

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:37.800
In 2025 the answer was about cellphones and rule violations.

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:45.300
In 2027 the brothers have to show that something has changed since then, because that is the only reason the hearing got moved up in the first place.

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:48.400
I am not going to tell you how that should come out.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:58.100
There are two dead people at the center of this, and there are two men who have spent more than three decades in prison, and the record gives you reasons to feel strongly in both directions.

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:02.400
The next hearing is tentatively scheduled for March 2027, and I will be watching it.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.500
The filings, the rulings and the family's statements are all public.

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:07.200
Read the file.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:08.500
Form your own conclusion.

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:09.700
That's it for this one.

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:11.200
Thanks for hanging out with me.

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:14.300
This has been CrimeTimeSnacks, and I'll catch you next time.
